PROTO- AGI

Proto-AGI architecture is designed to emulate artificial general intelligence (AGI) through the integration of diverse components and knowledge frameworks. A recent manuscript introduces a novel and holistic architecture for the ProtoAGI system, conceptualized from a systems engineering standpoint. This architecture aims to enhance the system’s performance and adaptability by integrating Large Language Models (LLMs), task-specific solvers, and a comprehensive knowledge repository.
The architecture includes several key components:
Large Language Models (LLMs): These models form the backbone of the system, providing the ability to process complex user inputs and generate contextually relevant responses.
Task-Specific Solvers: These solvers are designed to handle specific tasks, enabling the system to perform a wide range of functions beyond just language processing.
Comprehensive Knowledge Repository: This repository stores and organizes information, allowing the system to learn from historical datasets and adapt its responses based on this knowledge.
Additionally, the architecture emphasizes the importance of several features:
Adaptive Learning: The system is designed to learn from historical data, improving its performance over time.
Contextual Understanding: It processes intricate user inputs and generates responses that are contextually relevant.
Interconnected Components: The system integrates various components to work together seamlessly, enhancing its overall functionality.
A key aspect of this architecture is the interaction between LLMs, as shown in Fig. 2 from the manuscript. The Task Interpreter (TI) serves as the frontal lobe of ProtoAGI, where user inputs are deciphered. It employs a sophisticated Model of Experts (MoE) and LLM configuration to interpret user queries and tap into the Internal Knowledge Structure (IKS) for accurate responses.
This approach not only signifies a pioneering venture into the realm of AGI system design but also lays the groundwork for subsequent advancements in this field.






